\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}

\taxon{Example}
\title{A mixed-sign isometry has no norm-one Spin lift}

\p{On #{\mathbb R^2}, let #{Q(a,b)=a^2-b^2},
#{e_+=(1,0)}, and #{e_-=(0,1)}. The corresponding reflections are

##{r_{e_+}=\operatorname{diag}(-1,1),\qquad
r_{e_-}=\operatorname{diag}(1,-1),}

so #{g=r_{e_+}r_{e_-}=-I} lies in #{SO(Q)}. Its spinor norm is

##{\nu_Q(g)=[Q(e_+)Q(e_-)]=[-1]\ne[1].}

With the two norm conventions of \ref{fcap-0028}, the even Clifford lift
#{x=\widehat e_+\widehat e_-} has

##{N_{\mathrm{rev}}(x)=N_*(x)=-1.}

For every #{c\in\mathbb R^\times}, the scalar multiple #{cx} has norm
#{-c^2}, which cannot equal #{1}. More generally, any two Lipschitz lifts of
#{g} differ by a scalar by \ref{fcap-002D}, so no lift can be normalized into
the norm-one Spin carrier. Equivalently, the image theorem in
\ref{fcap-0023} excludes a Spin lift because #{\nu_Q(g)} is nontrivial.}

\p{This is the mixed-sign counterpart to the successful square
normalizations in \ref{fcap-000B} and \ref{fcap-000C}. Meinrenken notes that
over #{\mathbb R} one sometimes uses the weaker carrier condition
#{N(x)=\pm1}; under that different convention this #{x} passes the norm test
\citet{Remark 3.2, p. 52}{meinrenken2013clifford}. TauCeti's Spin group uses
the norm-one carrier, so the two conventions must not be identified.}
